 "captions": {
  "script": "These rocks move by themselves. And nobody pushes them. Glitch Diary, entry seven. Racetrack Playa, Death Valley. Stones up to three hundred twenty kilograms leave trails across a bone-dry lakebed. Scientists have studied them since the nineteen forties, and nobody saw one move. But that's not the strange part. A team planted rocks with GPS trackers and set up cameras. In December twenty thirteen, they finally caught it. A pond, seven centimetres deep, froze at night into windowpane ice, thinner than five millimetres. And here's what nobody expected. On a sunny day, the ice cracked into floating panels, and a gentle breeze pushed them, bulldozing hundreds of rocks a few metres per minute. These rocks may move about one millionth of the time. Would you have walked right past it? Tell me in the comments. Follow for the next entry of my glitch diary. Because these rocks are still moving...",
